Inkless Kafka is Aiven's Apache Kafka fork introducing an storage sub-system for _Diskless_ topics.
Compared to _Classic_ topics, Diskless topics do not rely on the local filesystem and Kafka replication, 
but move that responsibility to a distributed remote storage system (e.g. AWS S3).

It does this on the promise of reducing costs (highly driven by Networking costs when crossing zones)
and operational complexity (no rebalancing for diskless topics, faster scalability).

> This fork is an implementation for the KIP-1150 proposal which is being voted by the community,
> and should inspire the upstreaming of this feature if adopted.

Having worked on this codebase for more than a year now and iterated on some of its features,
it feels like a good time to start sharing more about it.
Specially now that we are working on integrating the Inkless sub-system with Kafka Tiered Storage.

## Terminology

Some common concepts to understand Inkless better:

- Topics, Partitions, and Replicas
- Records and Record Batches
- Local Log segment files and Remote shared WAL segment files

## Overall architecture

The Inkless sub-system is built on two main components:
A _data plane_ component in charge of the partition's read and write paths to remote storage,
enabling brokers to access _any topic partition_ without relying on Kafka's partition leadership or replication.
And the _control plane_ component to centralize the metadata storage (batch coordinates, remote files, etc.)
// this part feels a bit weak
to ensure Kafka's log semantics: append-only, sequential order. // maybe let's elaborate a bit more here.
In its current version, the control plane is backed by a Postgres database.

