## Performance and Cost considerations

Remote, AWS S3-like, storage systems on their standard multi-AZ versions
(I will not cover their single-zone variants)
tend to be cheap on storage capacity; but penalize high request rates.
This means that the smaller the files, the more PUT requests to upload them.

Similarly, the more fragmented the objects (e.g., many partitions on the same object) 
leads to potentially more GET requests to access different ranges from the same file.

The price of GET requests are usually 10% of PUT requests; 
so even though cheaper, you don't want to overlook into this difference.

This starts painting the ideal case for Inkless:

### Optimized for throughput

Large batches mean faster time to WAL object limit, reducing latency.
Large batches mean less batches per WAL object, reducing costs.
Large batches mean less batch metadata to commit, reducing load on Metadata storage.
Large batches mean less batches needed to build fetch responses, reducing latency.

